<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Marie, Countess of Champagne</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1145–1198</span> · <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a> · Also known as Marie de Champagne</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Marie was countess of Champagne by her marriage to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-i-count-of-champagne">Henry the Liberal</a>, and the eldest daughter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-vii-of-france">Louis VII of France</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eleanor-of-aquitaine">Eleanor of Aquitaine</a>. She acted as regent for her sons and was a leading literary patron, for whom <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chretien-de-troyes">Chrétien de Troyes</a> wrote his Lancelot and Andreas Capellanus his treatise on courtly love. Her court at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/troyes">Troyes</a> made Champagne a centre of French literature.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/marie-countess-of-champagne.jpg" alt="Marie, Countess of Champagne" width="246" height="354" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Henri d'Arbois de Jubainville · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Marie_Champagne.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
